# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
#
# The app answers at /app, and the file behind it is still app.html.
#
# A 200 is a rewrite, not a redirect: the browser keeps /app in the address bar
# and the bytes come from app.html. That distinction is the whole point of
# doing it this way. src/core/paths.js derives APP_ROOT from `document.baseURI`
# with `new URL(".", …)`, so the trailing slash decides where the app thinks it
# lives — /app resolves APP_ROOT to `/` and everything (sw.js, the manifest,
# every lazily-loaded stylesheet) keeps working, while /app/ would resolve it
# to `/app/` and move all of them a directory down. Do not add a slash here.
#
# Cloudflare Pages also serves an extensionless path for any .html asset by
# default, which would make this rule redundant. It is written out anyway: a
# default that no file in the repository records is a behaviour nobody can
# check, and this one decides whether the site loads at all.
#
# /app.html itself keeps working — Pages 301s the .html form to the clean one,
# and every link that was ever shared uses it.
/app  /app.html  200

# Only one other rule, and it exists for a shape of URL that people will keep typing
# for years: the site lived at github.io/RealtimeClipboard/ for its whole life
# before this domain, so the repo name is baked into every link that was ever
# shared, every bookmark, and every search result. GitHub itself 301s those to
# the apex with the prefix stripped — but only for as long as the old Pages site
# exists, and it is a tombstone now (.github/workflows/tombstone.yml). This
# catches anyone who reconstructs the old shape against the new host.
#
# :splat carries the rest of the path through, so /RealtimeClipboard/help/ lands
# on /help/ rather than on the homepage. A redirect that drops the path is worse
# than no redirect: it looks like it worked and silently loses the destination.
/RealtimeClipboard/*  /:splat  301

# NOT here: www -> apex. `_redirects` matches paths, not hostnames, so it cannot
# express it. Both hostnames are attached to the same Pages project and would
# otherwise serve the same content at two URLs. The redirect is a zone-level
# Redirect Rule in the Cloudflare dashboard instead — see docs/SEO.md §7.
